>> title: Dashi
>> category: Soups
>> tags: japanese, kombu, katsuobushi, stock, quick
>> counters: Ramen Shop, Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: dashi, ichiban dashi, ichiban-dashi, 出汁, だし, first dashi, kombu and bonito stock, Japanese soup stock, sea stock, awase dashi
>> pairs-with: shio-tare, shoyu-tare, shio-ramen
>> servings: 6
>> time: 45 min
>> slack: unforgiving — boiled kombu turns the pot slimy and faintly bitter, and there is no taking it back out of the broth
>> step.1: Everything here that asks for dashi means this one: 45 minutes, and 30 of them are a soak.
>> step.2: soak cold 30 min
>> step.3: heat to 150°F (65°C), lift the kombu out
>> step.4: steep 2 min off the heat, strain
>> step.5: Keep the spent kombu and flakes: simmered again in fresh water for 10 min they give niban dashi, the plainer second stock a miso soup is properly built on.

Everything on this counter that asks for dashi means this one. It takes forty-five minutes and thirty of them are a soak.

Soak @kombu{1%piece}(6 in, 15 g; wiped with a dry cloth, never rinsed) in @cold water{6%cups}(1.4 L) in a #saucepan{} ~soak{30%min}, until the sheet has softened and gone slippery to a fingertip.

Heat @&(~1)kombu water{} slowly to 150°F (65°C) and hold it there ~steep{10%min}, then lift the kombu out. The surface should shiver and never bubble: boiled kombu turns the pot slimy and faintly bitter, and there is no taking it back out.

Steep @katsuobushi{1%cup}(10 g; loosely packed) in @&(~1)kombu broth{} off the heat ~steep{2%min}, until every flake has sunk, then pour it through a fine sieve without pressing on the flakes.

Keep the spent kombu and flakes. Simmered again in fresh water for ten minutes they give niban dashi, which is a second, plainer stock and what a miso soup is properly built on.
